==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|tempten}}, from {{der|en|fro|tempter}} (French: {{m|fr|tenter}}), from {{der|en|la|tempto|temptare}}, from {{m|la|tento|tentare|to handle, touch, try, test, tempt}}, frequentative of {{m|la|teneo|tenere|to hold}}. Displaced native {{cog|en|costning||temptation}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|/tɛmpt/|/tɛmt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-tempt.ogg|Audio (GA)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɛmpt|ɛmt|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[provoke]] someone to do [[wrong]], especially by [[promising]] a [[reward]]; to [[entice]].
#: {{ux|en|She '''tempted''' me to eat the apple.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[attract]]; to [[allure]].
#: {{ux|en|Its glossy skin '''tempted''' me.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[provoke]] something; to [[court]].
#: {{ux|en|It would be '''tempting''' fate.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|provoke someone to do wrong}} {{l|en|entice}}, {{l|en|fand}}, {{l|en|lure}}, {{l|en|pander}}, {{l|en|tease}}
* {{sense|attract; allure}} {{l|en|beguile}}, {{l|en|entrance}}; see also [[Thesaurus:allure]]
* {{sense|provoke something}} {{l|en|foment}}, {{l|en|urge}}; see also [[Thesaurus:incite]]

